// **Five built-in traits have no trait object**, and `dyn Name` on them is refused where it is
// written (E0014) rather than naming a type nothing can inhabit.
//
// A trait object is a value plus a method to call on it, so the refusal follows from the trait's
// own contract: `Clone`, `Serialize` and `Deserialize` are markers whose behavior is synthesized
// and impose no method to dispatch; `From::from` is `static`, called on the type rather than on a
// value, so there is no receiver to erase; and `From`, `To`, `Serialize` and `Deserialize` all take
// a type argument that `dyn Name` has no syntax to carry — a `dyn To` would not say *to what*.
//
// Pinned so "refused" and "silently broken" cannot be confused later: every other built-in trait
// coerces in every position (see `dyn_builtin_trait_positions`), and the ones that do not say so.
// expect: error E0014 at 18:9
// expect: error E0014 at 19:9
// expect: error E0014 at 20:9
// expect: error E0014 at 21:9
// expect: error E0014 at 22:9
// expect: exit 1
fn a(x: dyn Clone): int { return 1 }
fn b(x: dyn From): int { return 2 }
fn c(x: dyn To): int { return 3 }
fn d(x: dyn Serialize): int { return 4 }
fn e(x: dyn Deserialize): int { return 5 }
echo 0
